      --scope system --local-dir ./bundles

Use "tt binary install <command> --help" for more information about a command.

=== tt binary install tarantool --help
Install Tarantool Community or Enterprise Edition and its headers.

Bare versions select Community Edition: 3, 3.8, or 3.8.0.
Tarantool Community Edition installation supports version 3.0 and newer.

On Linux amd64/arm64 with glibc, use prebuilt Community release binaries when
available.
Other platforms and releases without prebuilt packages are built from source
automatically.
Use --source-build to always compile Community releases from source.

EE suffix selects Enterprise Edition: 3.8.1ee or 3ee.

Enterprise archive names such as gc64-3.8.0-0-r94 also select Enterprise
Edition.
Use --dev for Enterprise development builds, stored as
dev_<full-archive-version>
with their revision, separately from releases.
Use "tt binary search tarantool" or "tt binary search tarantool-ee" to list
versions.

Omitting the version selects the latest Community release. Git commits, master,
and pull requests also select Community Edition. Use pr/<number> for a pull
request.

With --local-repo <dir>, <dir>/tarantool
must be a local Git clone of the Tarantool source repository.
Use --local-dir <dir> to install Enterprise SDK archives from a local directory.

Git commits, master, and pull requests are built from source automatically.
The --local-repo, --dynamic, and --use-docker options also select source builds.

Use --from-build <dir> to link an existing Community or Enterprise build into
the selected scope without copying or rebuilding it. Use --include-dir to
select the headers directory. --from-build accepts no version argument.

[0;1;39mUSAGE[0m
  tt binary install tarantool [version|commit|pull-request] [flags]

  tt binary install tarantool [version|commit|pull-request] [flags]
[0;1;39m
FLAGS[0m
      --credentials-file string   File containing customer-zone login and
                                  password on separate lines
      --dev                       Install a development build (Enterprise
                                  Edition only)
      --dynamic                   Build Tarantool with dynamic linking
      --from-build string         Link Tarantool from an existing build
                                  directory
  -h, --help                      help for tarantool
      --include-dir string        Path to the Tarantool headers directory
                                  (requires --from-build)
      --local-dir string          Directory with Tarantool Enterprise SDK
                                  archives (tarantool-enterprise-sdk-*.tar.gz)
      --local-repo string         Directory containing the Tarantool Git source
                                  repository in tarantool/
      --source-build              Always build Tarantool Community releases from
                                  source
      --use-docker                Build Tarantool in an Ubuntu Docker container
[0;1;39m
GLOBAL FLAGS[0m
  -f, --force          Skip dependency checks
      --no-clean       Keep temporary files
      --reinstall      Reinstall an existing version
      --scope string   Scope: project, user or system (default "project")
  -V, --verbose        Show diagnostic output and build commands
[0;1;39m
EXAMPLES[0m
  # Build and install Tarantool from the local repository.
  $ tt binary install tarantool --local-repo ./distfiles

  # Link an existing Tarantool build into the project scope.
  $ tt binary install tarantool \
      --from-build ~/src/tarantool/build

  # Link a build with custom headers into the user scope.
  $ tt binary install tarantool --scope user \
      --from-build ~/src/tarantool/build \
      --include-dir ~/src/tarantool/include

  # Build Tarantool with two parallel jobs.
  $ MAKEFLAGS="-j2" tt binary install tarantool 3.4.0 --source-build

  # Install the latest Enterprise release in minor version 3.8.
  $ tt binary install tarantool 3.8ee

