=== tt cluster topology --help
Discover and print the current cluster topology by connecting to cluster nodes
via net.box.

The cluster configuration can be loaded from a file, etcd, or Tarantool Config
Storage. It determines the instances and their listen URIs. Each instance is
contacted and the live topology is merged into one view.

<APP> names an application tt runs, found as the instance commands find it, with
--scope and --template: the cluster configuration is the one of its pair, and a
relative unix socket is taken against the application directory its instances
run in. With -c a relative unix socket is taken against the directory of the
configuration file.

JSON output contains replicasets keyed by UUID and their instances
(instance_uuid, instance_name, hostname, mode, status). If a replicaset is
unreachable and its UUID is not configured, its name is used as the key.
Unreachable instances have status "not reachable".

The URL specifies a etcd or tarantool config storage connection settings in the
following format:
http(s)://[username:password@]host:port/prefix[?arguments]

* prefix - a base path to Tarantool configuration in etcd or tarantool config
  storage.

Possible arguments:
* key - a target configuration key in the prefix.
* name - a name of an instance in the cluster configuration.
* timeout - a request timeout in seconds (default 3.0).
* ssl_key_file - a path to a private SSL key file.
* ssl_cert_file - a path to an SSL certificate file.
* ssl_ca_file - a path to a trusted certificate authorities (CA) file.
* ssl_ca_path - a path to a trusted certificate authorities (CA) directory.
* ssl_ciphers - a list of allowed SSL ciphers.
* verify_host - set off (default true) verification of the certificate’s name
  against the host.
* verify_peer - set off (default true) verification of the peer’s SSL
  certificate.

The command supports the following environment variables:
* TT_CLI_USERNAME - specifies a Tarantool username;
* TT_CLI_PASSWORD - specifies a Tarantool password.
* TT_CLI_ETCD_USERNAME - specifies a Etcd username;
* TT_CLI_ETCD_PASSWORD - specifies a Etcd password.

The priority of credentials:
environment variables < command flags < URL credentials.

HTTPS connection behavior:
* The https:// scheme enables TLS for both etcd and Tarantool Config Storage
  (TCS), even when no ssl_* URL arguments are specified.
* For etcd, certificate verification is skipped if neither ssl_ca_file nor
  ssl_ca_path is specified. A configured CA enables verification unless
  explicitly disabled.
* TCS keeps the certificate verification behavior of its TLS backend. The
  default Go backend uses system CAs unless ssl_ca_file is specified.
* Do not mix http:// and https:// addresses in an etcd endpoint list. If any
  endpoint uses https://, TLS is enabled for all endpoints.

[0;1;39mUSAGE[0m
  tt cluster topology (<APP> | -c <CLUSTER_CONFIG|URI>) [flags]
[0;1;39m
FLAGS[0m
  -c, --config string              path or URI of the cluster configuration
      --connect-timeout duration   timeout for establishing a binary connection
                                   (default 1s)
      --format string              output format: table or json (default
                                   "table")
  -h, --help                       help for topology
  -p, --password string            password for the URI case
      --scope string               scope the application is looked for in:
                                   project, user or system
      --sslcafile string           path to a trusted certificate authorities
                                   (CA) file for the URI case
      --sslcertfile string         path to an SSL certificate file for the URI
                                   case
      --sslciphers string          colon-separated (:) list of SSL cipher suites
                                   for the URI case
      --sslkeyfile string          path to a private SSL key file for the URI
                                   case
      --sslpassword string         password for decrypting the private SSL key
                                   file for a cluster connection
      --sslpasswordfile string     path to a file containing the private SSL key
                                   password for a cluster connection
      --template string            template of the application the instances are
                                   described in (default "default")
  -u, --username string            username for the URI case

