=== tt connect --help
Connect to the tarantool instance.

The command supports the following Tarantool environment variables:
* TT_CLI_USERNAME - specifies a Tarantool username
* TT_CLI_PASSWORD - specifies a Tarantool password

You could pass command line arguments to the interpreted SCRIPT or COMMAND
passed via -f flag:

echo "print(...)" | tt connect user:pass@localhost:3013 -f- 1, 2, 3

[0;1;39mUSAGE[0m
  tt connect (<APP>:<INSTANCE> | <URI>) [flags] [-f <FILE>] [-- ARGS]
  COMMAND | tt connect (<APP>:<INSTANCE> | <URI>) [flags]
  COMMAND | tt connect (<APP>:<INSTANCE> | <URI>) [flags] [-f-] [-- ARGS]

  The URI can be specified in the following formats:
  * [tcp://][username:password@][host:port]
  * [unix://][username:password@]socketpath
  To specify relative path without `unix://` use `./`.

  Available commands:
  * \shortcuts - get the full list of available shortcuts
  * \set language <language> - set language (lua or sql)
  * \set output <format> - set output format (lua[,line|block] or yaml)
  * \set delimiter <delimiter> - set expression delimiter
  * \help - show available backslash commands
  * \quit - quit interactive console
[0;1;39m
FLAGS[0m
      --binary                     connect to instance using binary port
      --connect-timeout duration   timeout for establishing a binary connection
                                   (default 1s)
  -f, --file string                file to read the script for evaluation. "-" -
                                   read the script from stdin
  -h, --help                       help for connect
  -i, --interactive                enter interactive mode after executing 'FILE'
  -l, --language string            language: lua or sql
  -x, --outputformat string        output format: yaml, lua, table or ttable
                                   (default "yaml")
  -p, --password string            password
      --scope string               scope the application is looked for in:
                                   project, user or system
      --sslcafile string           path to a trusted certificate authorities
                                   (CA) file
      --sslcertfile string         path to an SSL certificate file
      --sslciphers string          colon-separated (:) list of SSL cipher suites
                                   the connection
      --sslkeyfile string          path to a private SSL key file
      --sslpassword string         password for decrypting the private SSL key
                                   file
      --sslpasswordfile string     path to a file containing the password for
                                   decrypting the private SSL key file
      --template string            template of the application the instances are
                                   described in (default "default")
  -u, --username string            username

