=== tt instance start --help
Start the instances the targets select. By default every instance runs in the
background under a watchdog of tt, which restarts it as the restart field of its
record says.

With --foreground tt stays in the foreground, for systemd, a container or a
terminal, while every instance runs under a watchdog of its own that does not
restart it. The output of the instances goes to their tt.log and to the standard
output, prefixed with the instance when there are several. SIGINT, SIGTERM and
SIGQUIT stop every instance, as a stop of its watchdog does; tt instance stop
stops one of them from another terminal. An instance that ends does not stop the
others. tt exits once all of them have ended: with 0 when they were stopped in
time, with the exit code of the first instance that failed otherwise, 137 for
one killed as it did not stop in time.

[0;1;39mUSAGE[0m
  tt instance start [<APP>:<INSTANCE> | <APP>:*][,...] [flags]
[0;1;39m
FLAGS[0m
      --foreground        stay in the foreground while the instances run,
                          without restarting them
  -h, --help              help for start
      --scope string      scope the application is looked for in: project, user
                          or system
      --template string   template of the application the instances are
                          described in (default "default")

=== tt instance status --help
The 'status' command provides information about the status of the instances.

Columns:
- INSTANCE: The name of the instance, <APP>:<INSTANCE>.
- STATUS: The current status of the instance:
	- RUNNING: The instance is up and running.
	- NOT RUNNING: The instance is not running.
	- ERROR: The process has terminated unexpectedly.
- UNIT: The systemd unit that manages the instance, for an instance that
  tt service systemd install put under a unit. The column is shown only when
  a unit manages some of the instances.
- PID: The watchdog process PID.
- MODE: The mode of the instance, indicating its read/write status:
	- RO: The instance is in read-only mode.
	- RW: The instance is in read-write mode.
- CONFIG: The config info status (for Tarantool 3+).
- BOX: The box info status.
- UPSTREAM: The replication upstream status.

MODE, CONFIG, BOX and UPSTREAM are what a running Tarantool instance tells
over its console socket; an external process shows the state of its process
alone. --details prints the alerts of the instances, and the tarantool each
Tarantool instance starts on and where it comes from.

[0;1;39mUSAGE[0m
  tt instance status [<APP>:<INSTANCE> | <APP>:*][,...] [flags]
[0;1;39m
FLAGS[0m
  -d, --details                     print detailed alerts and the tarantool the
                                    instances start on
  -o, --format string               output format: json, yaml, table,
                                    pretty-table (default "table")
  -h, --help                        help for status
      --instance-timeout duration   timeout for collecting a single instance's
                                    status; 0 disables the timeout (default 5s)
      --scope string                scope the application is looked for in:
                                    project, user or system
      --template string             template of the application the instances
                                    are described in (default "default")

