=== tt package list --help
Show the packages present in the chosen scope: in the project scope the
project's own package plus every guest installed from an archive, read from the
metadata install records under .rocks/manifests/; in the user and system scopes
every application installed under <root>/versions/, with the version
<root>/apps/<name> selects and every installed one. Nothing is fetched and
nothing is resolved. This is what is on disk — not what the current manifest
declares, which is what tt package deps reports. --tree additionally shows each
package's dependencies and, for every one of them, whether another package holds
it too — that is, whether it would be removed along with the package or stay; it
applies to the project scope, the versions of the user and system scopes sharing
no dependencies.

[0;1;39mUSAGE[0m
  tt package list [flags]

  tt package list [flags]
[0;1;39m
FLAGS[0m
  -o, --format string   output format: table, json or yaml (default "table")
  -h, --help            help for list
      --scope string    scope to list: project (the working directory), user or
                        system (the applications under the root tt binary uses)
                        (default "project")
      --tree            show each package's dependencies and which of them are
                        shared
[0;1;39m
GLOBAL FLAGS[0m
  -C, --directory string   work on the project in DIR, as if it were the current
                           directory

=== tt package pack --help
Run the full build and assemble its result into a reproducible .tt archive. By
default the archive is self-contained: it carries _runtime/ (Tarantool, tt and
TCM when [platform].tcm is set) and the whole dependency closure in .rocks/, so
installing it needs no network. --without-deps drops both. The project's
instance templates in .templates/ travel at the archive root in either mode;
each .templates/<name>/ must hold config.yaml, instances.yaml or both, and a
broken template fails the pack. A separate tt package build beforehand is not
needed. The archive path is printed to stdout.

[0;1;39mUSAGE[0m
  tt package pack [flags]

  tt package pack [flags]
[0;1;39m
FLAGS[0m
  -h, --help                   help for pack
      --locked                 fail if the lock is out of date instead of
                               re-resolving it
      --product string         product to pack (default: the product marked
                               default)
      --registry stringArray   rock server to use instead of the configured
                               ones: an http(s) URL or a local directory; repeat
                               to give an ordered list (overrides TT_REGISTRIES
                               and [platform].registries)
      --without-deps           pack without _runtime/ and without foreign
                               dependencies in .rocks/
[0;1;39m
GLOBAL FLAGS[0m
  -C, --directory string   work on the project in DIR, as if it were the current
                           directory

=== tt package remove --help
Remove NAME from [dependencies] and [dev_dependencies], then re-resolve
app.manifest.lock without it. The rocks that were only there to satisfy it drop
out of the closure with it; every other version is held. A name the manifest
does not declare is an error rather than a silent success — it is almost always
a typo — and so is a name only a component declares, since which component owns
a dependency is not this command's decision to unmake. Nothing is deleted from
.rocks/; tt package uninstall is what removes files.

[0;1;39mUSAGE[0m
  tt package remove NAME [flags]

  tt package remove NAME [flags]
[0;1;39m
