=== tt service systemd --help
Make the systemd units that run the instances of an application installed in the
system scope.

The template unit <APP>@.service runs one instance, tt instance start
--foreground <APP>:%i, with TT_SYSTEM_ROOT set to the root of the system scope,
as the user and the group --run-as names, tarantool by default. tt does not
restart the instance: systemd does, as Restart= says. The drop-in
<APP>@<INSTANCE>.service.d/tt.conf of every instance sets Restart= from the
restart field of its record and TimeoutStopSec= from its stop_timeout, with a
margin for tt, which stops the instance itself. ExecStart= runs <root>/bin/tt,
or the tt that makes the units when the system scope has none.

install records the instances in <root>/etc/<APP>/systemd.yaml: tt instance
start, stop, restart and kill refuse them and point at systemctl. tt does not
run systemctl itself.

<APP> names an application as the instance commands name it, and the units are
made for the system scope only: the user scope and projects are refused; inside
a project of the same name, --scope system picks the installed application.

[0;1;39mUSAGE[0m
  tt service systemd [flags] <command> [command flags]
[0;1;39m
COMMANDS[0m
  generate    Print the systemd units of an application
  install     Install the systemd units of an application
  uninstall   Remove the systemd units of an application
[0;1;39m
FLAGS[0m
  -h, --help   help for systemd

Use "tt service systemd <command> --help" for more information about a command.

=== tt service systemd generate --help
Print the systemd units of the application: the template unit and the drop-in of
every instance, each after a line ==> <file> <==. With --unit-dir the units are
written into the directory instead, which is created if need be. Nothing is
installed and nothing is recorded: tt instance commands do not leave the
instances to the units.

[0;1;39mUSAGE[0m
  tt service systemd generate <APP> [flags]
[0;1;39m
FLAGS[0m
  -h, --help                  help for generate
      --run-as user[:group]   the user and the group the units run the instances
                              as; the group is the user's name when left out
                              (default tarantool:tarantool)
      --scope string          scope the application is looked for in: only
                              system is supported
      --unit-dir string       write the units into this directory instead of
                              printing them

=== tt service systemd install --help
Write the systemd units of the application into the unit directory,
/etc/systemd/system unless --unit-dir names another, and record in
<root>/etc/<APP>/systemd.yaml that the units manage its instances. Run it again
after the instances of the application change: it rewrites the units and removes
the drop-ins of the instances that are gone. A unit file of the same name that
tt did not write is an error, and so is an instance that runs without a unit:
stop it with tt instance stop first.

--run-as is recorded with the units: without it, an install over installed units
keeps the user and the group they run as.

systemctl is not run: the command prints what to run.

[0;1;39mUSAGE[0m
  tt service systemd install <APP> [flags]
[0;1;39m
FLAGS[0m
  -h, --help                  help for install
      --run-as user[:group]   the user and the group the units run the instances
                              as; the group is the user's name when left out
                              (default tarantool:tarantool)
      --scope string          scope the application is looked for in: only
                              system is supported
      --unit-dir string       the directory the units are written into (default
                              "/etc/systemd/system")

=== tt service systemd uninstall --help
Remove the systemd units tt service systemd install wrote for the application,
from the directory it wrote them into, and the record in
<root>/etc/<APP>/systemd.yaml. The application itself is not needed. Units that
are not installed are no failure: there is nothing to remove. An instance that
runs under its unit is an error: stop the units first, with systemctl disable
--now. A file tt did not write is left in place.

